WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.494
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.277 --> 00:00:07.610
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Hey, everyone.

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:10.120
We're here at the Google
Cloud Next Developer Zone.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.550
This is full of cool little
showcases, hands-on labs,

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:15.520
tutorials, and of course, the
DevZone Stage, where people

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.370
are able to come here and
learn all about the cool things

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:20.950
but really get their hands on
them and play around with them.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:24.160
Speaking of which, we're at the
BQML station with Felipe Hoffa,

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.750
who loves data and BigQuery.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.250
So Felipe, tell me
a little bit more

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:28.540
about what we're
looking at here.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:29.498
FELIPE HOFFA: Hi, Mark.

00:00:29.498 --> 00:00:32.740
So what we have here is a
Stack Overflow Predictor.

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:35.458
How long will it take for
people to answer your question?

00:00:35.458 --> 00:00:37.750
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So what
does that mean, specifically?

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.430
So I can go into Stack
Overflow, I can ask a question,

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:42.700
but someone's not going
to respond immediately.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:43.492
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah.

00:00:43.492 --> 00:00:45.452
So I don't know
if you ever asked

00:00:45.452 --> 00:00:46.660
a question of Stack Overflow.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:47.780
It takes some time.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.940
And you never know
how long will it take.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:50.220
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Right.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:51.290
FELIPE HOFFA: Well,
with this tool,

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:53.470
we can kind of predict
how long will it take,

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:55.810
looking at all the
past data that we

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:57.310
have from Stack Overflow.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:58.220
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Now,
how do you do that?

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.770
Because BigQuery, you can
do a bunch of data analysis.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:02.640
But you can't build a machine
learning model in BigQuery,

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:03.140
right?

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.610
FELIPE HOFFA: Well, you can now.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:05.920
So I love BigQuery.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.570
I love the analytics.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.130
Thanks to the
public data program,

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:12.130
we have a copy of Stack
Overflow, the dump that they

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:13.960
publish every quarter.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:15.700
So we loaded that in
the query and then

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.310
you can start running your
own SQL queries over it.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:20.890
Anyone can come and run
any queries they want.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:23.330
They have a free
terabyte every month.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.750
So the first step
with data analytics

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.060
is just to look at
all your questions,

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:29.440
look at all your
answers, and get

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.890
an average of how long did
it take for people to answer

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:34.057
questions on Stack Overflow.

00:01:34.057 --> 00:01:36.640
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So people can
come here to the Next showcase

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.260
floor, play around
with this interface

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.705
here, change and see
if they ask a question,

00:01:40.705 --> 00:01:42.580
and then based on the
machine learning model,

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:45.340
they'll see about how long
they'd have to wait for someone

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:46.330
to go in and answer it.

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:47.247
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:01:47.247 --> 00:01:49.300
So how did we go
from the analytics

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.720
of just aggregate group
but to a prediction?

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.760
There is a lot of
spaces in between.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:59.740
Like, for example, to get an
answer for the Google BigQuery

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:03.520
tag at 8:00 AM for a
medium question that

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.190
ends with a-- doesn't end with
a question mark, that starts

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:10.509
with a Y. For someone that
created an account in 2017,

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:14.410
we're predicting that
it will take 53 minutes.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.010
Maybe they will
not get an answer,

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:19.980
but with an 85% probability
they will get one.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:25.030
And there is a chance of
being downvoted of about 7%

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:26.200
for these statistics.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.620
Now, how did we build this?

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:31.630
We created a linear
regression using

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:34.005
all of these features and the
data that we had available.

00:02:34.005 --> 00:02:36.380
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So with all
this different information,

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:38.850
you can know when you ask
a question approximately,

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:40.255
hopefully pretty
relatively, when

00:02:40.255 --> 00:02:41.380
it's going to be answered,.

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:42.820
But you could also
maybe even predict

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.205
when's the best time
to ask a question.

00:02:44.205 --> 00:02:44.890
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:46.390
If you want to go
the opposite way.

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:50.260
So let's say it takes
53 minutes on a Tuesday.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:55.070
On Sunday it takes more like
an hour to get an answer.

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:57.935
But there is a higher
chance of getting an answer.

00:02:57.935 --> 00:02:59.810
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
lots of cool information

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:00.760
to play around with here.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.850
But people can also come
here and actually learn

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.163
BQML hands-on, right?

00:03:04.163 --> 00:03:05.080
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:08.170
So the alternative for
people that are not here

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:11.800
to see how I made this, they can
go to my blog post on Medium.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:15.520
Just search for Felipe Hoffa,
When Will Stack Overflow Reply.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.100
And if you're here on Next,
we have code labs ready

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:21.410
for you to run that you
can also find online.

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:24.190
But there are so many
ways to learn and get

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:25.627
your hands into these tools.

00:03:25.627 --> 00:03:26.710
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Perfect.

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:28.690
So lots of cool stuff from BQML.

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:31.210
Come down to the floor,
get started, or check out

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:32.650
the resources online.

